                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "9de19f68-da1a-420d-ad79-838b2a35294a",
                        "entry_id": "8ad3dcbb-2191-3d10-8122-a110e22b1499",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "gemmae"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f20a14b1-db64-3940-9a65-db3c0bb36da1",
                        "entry_id": "8ad3dcbb-2191-3d10-8122-a110e22b1499",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An asexual reproductive structure, as found in animals such as hydra (genus Hydra) and plants such as liverworts (division Marchantiophyta), consisting of a cluster of cells from which new individuals can develop; a bud."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "3682c8a3-8a36-4163-9940-b73f63a5c000",
                                "sense_id": "f20a14b1-db64-3940-9a65-db3c0bb36da1",
                                "text": "I know of no other genera with such intramarginal formation of true gemmae."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0d5165eb-accb-48bb-ae58-7229777ca8bd",
                                "sense_id": "f20a14b1-db64-3940-9a65-db3c0bb36da1",
                                "text": "Gemmae are frequently longer than wide or of irregular shape.\nAccording to Degenkolbe, gemmae-bearing leaves are always different in form from normal leaves."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "83b2d88c-317b-4aa3-9bb0-be5762193d43",
                                "sense_id": "f20a14b1-db64-3940-9a65-db3c0bb36da1",
                                "text": "In Marchantia polymorpha, high temperature promotes germination of gemmae (Dacknowski, 1907), and heat absorbed by the gemmae accelerates their germination (Fitting, 1942)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": [
